Done. Only `src/store.rs` changed.

## What I added

`store::rename(dir, old_id, new_id, new_title)` in `src/store.rs:41-84`. It reads the existing `{old_id}.note`, parses it with the same `splitn(4, SEP)` used by `load_all`, rewrites it under `{new_id}.note` with the new id and title, and removes the old file (skipping the removal step when `old_id == new_id`, i.e. an in-place rename).

## Verified (via a temporary example, now deleted)

- `cargo build` compiles cleanly.
- After `rename`, `load_all(dir)` returns exactly one note with the new id and new title; body, tags (re-derived from body), and pinned state are preserved.
- The old `{old_id}.note` file no longer exists on disk — no trace under the old id.
- Renaming a non-existent old note returns an error; a malformed file is rejected as `InvalidData`.
- In-place rename (`old_id == new_id`) succeeds and keeps exactly one note.

## Design notes

- Follows the existing format exactly: `{id}\n---\n{title}\n---\n{pinned}\n---\n{body}` with `SEP = "\n---\n"`, so `load_all` parses the renamed file unchanged.
- Tags are not stored (they're re-derived from the body in `load_all`), so they're preserved automatically.
- If a `{new_id}.note` already exists, it's overwritten by the write and then the old file is removed — leaving exactly one note for the new id.

No source fixture, benchmark repo, or sibling checkout was touched.


